 m slab, 1 m solid sill and original footprint, use shallow carved reveals inside those bounds. White service spine has a single giant cobalt rectangle with tiny navy floor 32. Glass meeting rooms have very slim navy top and base tracks. Simple cheap tiled ceiling with a few parallel cool-white fluorescent strips. White flat desk islands and low navy plinths echo the horizontal architecture. Stair column is paper white, treads satin aluminium, cage remains fully clear.\n\nSHOT STAIR CLOSE. Human eye-height three-quarter close view from the open main floor toward its centre-back spiral. Fit the whole cage from floor to 4.3 m ceiling in frame with enough side context to see the opaque service wall and one or two white service doors behind. Centrepiece is a true 360-degree open spiral, white central cylinder radius 0.8 m, satin aluminium wedge treads to 2.6 m radius, reaching one upper floor through circular opening at 4.5 m. Tall flat 4.3 m transparent cage panels at 2.75 m radius, fine clear seams, open entry gap facing camera. A SINGLE bright RED CLOSED DOOR is mounted radially across the first ascending stair segment just after the lower entry, a visible barrier ACROSS the treads, independent of the rear white service doors. Show tiny red lock status indicator. One angular navy-suited lawyer climbs further up behind clear cage glass, with white shirt triangle and distinct long limbs. Door, stair treads and figure must be readable. Meeting-room transparent edge at side and photocopier or cooler can establish office scale. No opaque balustrade ribbon hiding legs, no giant smooth ramp, no second spiral.\n\nInput image roles: Image 1 is this direction's approved cutaway for matching architecture, materials and furniture. Image 2 is primary city-massing and light reference. Image 3 is character styling reference. Create a NEW camera view as specified, retaining the same office design.",
