ndicator. It is a stair gate, separate from the three service doors. Clearly show the ascending treads, glass cage and red gate. A dark-suited figure climbs visibly behind clear cage glass.\nRear spine contains three separate opaque rooms WC, STORE, UTIL behind the rear wall, each accessed from main floor by one white 1.2 x 2.4 m service door. Use those tiny labels if seen. One or two small glass meeting rooms sit to the left and right on the main floor, 2.5 m high partitions with open air above them to 4.3 m ceiling. Keep sight through them toward stair entrance. Sparse white desk clusters, dark chairs, early flat-panel monitors, low storage, a beige-grey office photocopier and water cooler create low cover. Wide empty circulation, long sightlines, no maze.\nAcross the 12 m street gap stands a mirrored rival tower with matching bay rhythm and floor heights, bright white interior backing behind dark suited figures. White-blue block city and saturated blue sky with pale atmospheric haze beyond.\nPalette: dominant Paper White #F3F4F4, Silver Grey #C9CED3, restrained Deep Navy #111B38 dark anchors, Corporate Cobalt #244FB9 only as one or two large confident graphic areas, Cyan #20D4E6 only tiny indicators or a thin line. Grey-blue carpet, cheap modular acoustic ceiling tiles and cool fluorescent strips, clean satin aluminium. Red appears only on stair's locked gate. Daylight and cool broad ambient fill, white stays cool white.\nAvoid plants, planters, greenery, blinds, mirror glass, frosting, decorative bars across windows, cyberpunk, greeble, exposed pipes, random panels, grime, pastel vaporwave, rounded bubble furniture, tiny scattered blue ornaments, real logos, copied signage. The open spiral stair is the centrepiece and people remain readable through multiple glass layers.\n\nDirection COBALT PORTAL. A single strong architectural graphic is a huge flat cobalt portal on the REAR SERVICE WALL, framing the clear spiral in front of it. Two broad blue vertical piers painted on the wall and a blue upper lintel/soffit band form one squared U around a white centre, with oversize navy 32 in the white centre. Keep its piers on the existing opaque rear wall, never around or over the cage, preserve all service doorways and the 4.3 m clear ceiling. The rest stays almost entirely paper white and muted grey-blue, slim silver meeting partitions, slim white desks, tiled ceiling and quiet cool strip lights. External facade continues the portal idea as a single cobalt rectangular wall graphic on one broad existing opaque pier, with white horizontal bands and clear glazing. This is large-scale flat transport-architecture graphics, with no extra freestanding walls or physical arch blocking a sightline. Stair column remains white.\n\nSHOT CUTAWAY. A beautiful complete roofless dollhouse view from 40 degrees above, camera out over the street looking into the entire office. Rectangular floor runs horizontally across image, STREET AND SEVEN WINDOW BAYS ALONG NEAR/FRONT EDGE, full-width three-room service spine ALONG FAR/BACK EDGE. The front glass wall is sectioned low for visibility but retain an explicit continuous 1 m white cover sill and the bay division columns. Show full floor bounds. Roof removed, show just a narrow rear-edge strip of the designed ceiling. At far rear show roofless WC, STORE and UTIL with their equipment and three doors in front wall. Open spiral cage stands in front of rear wall, never inside service spine, centred left-right. Two transparent meeting rooms flank the foreground-left and foreground-right with wide centre sightline. Desks and mundane office equipment near side walls leave spacious paths. Include dark suited scale figures, one on stair. Rival facade across street may be a faint sliver along image bottom, never obstruct floor. Maintain 28-to-18 overall floor aspect and the 14-to-4 depth split. Architectural concept painting, no dimensions or diagram overlays.\n\nInput image roles: Image1 is GEOMETRY AND CAMERA ONLY reference for exact shared floor, three rear service rooms, spiral position, 1m front sills and meeting-room proportions. REDESIGN its architectural treatment according to this direction. Image2 is the primary massing/lighting reference. Image3 is suit proportions only. Rear spine must have exactly three rooms spanning8m/12m/8m, with only TWO internal dividers. Red gate should be radial from centre column to cage across stair treads, with open entry gap. For ceiling-led direction show roof removed over the front two-thirds but retain a rear third CEILING STRIP with actual luminous large square panels visible on its underside, just enough to communicate the lighting concept while keeping whole floor legible.",
